<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Yoreh Day'ah, Volume 1</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Laws of Chailev [Forbidden Fat] and Dam [Blood]</div>
<div class="halacha-title">4</div>

<div class="subtitle">
  <em>Chailev and Dam</em> — in which will be elucidated the Laws of Pesach, the matter of the
  prohibition of chametz, the mitzva of eating matzah, and the matter of the Counting [of the Omer].
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Title]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">חֵלֶב וָדָם, וּבוֹ יִתְבָּאֵר הִלְכוֹת פֶּסַח וְעִנְיַן אִסּוּר חָמֵץ וּמִצְוַת אֲכִילַת מַצָּה וְעִנְיַן סְפִירָה</span>
</details>

<hr class="divider"/>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════ OPENING SOURCE ══════════════════════════ -->

<div class="source-line">
  Based upon the Torah-teaching <em>"And G-d led them around…"</em> [<span class="ht">Vayasev Elohim</span>],
  Siman 62 [in Likutay Moharan, Volume 1] — see there the entire Torah-teaching.
</div>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
The general principle, in brief, as it pertains to our matter: through the eating of Israel, a
<span class="kab">yichud</span> [unification] of the
<span class="kab">Kudsha Brich Hu</span> [the Holy One, Blessed be He] and His
<span class="kab">Shechintay</span> [Divine Presence],
<span class="kab">apin b'apin</span> [face to face], is effected — and so on.
However, the eating must be from food that has been <em>clarified</em>
[<span class="ht">nitbarar</span>], for from food that contains admixture, it is possible to sin
through such eating. And the clarification of foods comes about through
<span class="em">faith</span> [<span class="ht">emunah</span>].

For there are difficulties [<span class="ht">kushyos</span>] that can be resolved, and so on; and there are
difficulties that cannot be resolved or answered until the Future [the <span class="ht">Messianic era</span>], and
so on — regarding those, one must simply stand firm in faith. And even the difficulties that do have
answers, sometimes the pathways of intellect become blocked and one does not know how to answer them,
and so on. The remedy for this is the study of the <span class="ht">Poskim</span> [halachic decisors], and so on.
But the difficulties for which there is no resolution, it is forbidden for a person to dwell on them —
he must only strengthen himself in faith. And when one has faith in completeness, he can attain through
his eating a <span class="kab">yichud</span> of the <span class="kab">Kudsha Brich Hu</span> and His
<span class="kab">Shechinta</span>, and so on; and to complete the faith, and so on.

One must first elevate the sparks [<span class="ht">nitzotzos</span>] of the letters of speech.
And when the letters of speech are clarified, the speech itself turns and gathers the holy sparks from
among the <span class="ht">klippos</span> [husks of impurity], and so on. And in order to elevate the
sparks of the letters of speech, fasting is required, and so on, for through fasting one cleaves to the
<em>back</em> of holiness, and so on, for fasting is of the aspect of
<em>returning backward</em> — to the beginning of the fast, and so on.

Moreover, the essence of all things is the beginning, for
<em>all beginnings are difficult</em>, since one is going from one extreme to its opposite, and so on.
It follows that the essence of the power of all the days of one's service [of G-d] is nothing other than
the <em>beginning</em> — to which we must return, back to day one, when one began to understand, to fast,
which was the start of teshuvah [repentance], which was difficult for him, that beginning, going as it
did from one extreme to its opposite. And each day one returns backward to receive strength from that
beginning, as above.

The general principle is that through fasting one cleaves to the back of holiness, and then the
<span class="kab">Mayim d'Chasadim</span> [Waters of Lovingkindness] are drawn forth to moisten the
constriction of the throat, from the aspect of <span class="verse">"my throat is parched"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 69:4]</span>, and so on, for then the three ministers of Pharaoh are subdued:
the <em>chief cupbearer</em>, the <em>chief baker</em>, and the <em>chief butcher</em> — who correspond to
the <em>trachea</em> [<span class="ht">kaneh</span>], the <em>esophagus</em> [<span class="ht">veshet</span>],
and the <em>veins/arteries</em> [<span class="ht">veridin</span>] — which are of the aspect of the
lust for eating and drinking, and so on; their sustenance comes from <em>Pharaoh</em>, who
[in his Hebrew letters] spells <span class="acro">הָעֹרֶף</span> [<em>ha-oref</em> — the back of the neck],
that is to say: from <em>behind</em>. And his primary nourishment comes from [corrupt] speech, and so on.

And when the letters of speech are clarified through fasting, then the <em>face</em> of holiness is
revealed, and so on, so as to extract the swallowed from their mouth — of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"[there is] a time when one man dominates another man to his own harm"</span>
<span class="src">[Koheles 8:9]</span>, and so on.
Then those distant from Hashem draw near to Him, and faith is completed in perfection, and is adorned
with adornments that were never before, and so on. And then his eating is exceedingly precious, for
through his eating the <span class="kab">Kudsha Brich Hu</span> and His <span class="kab">Shechinta</span>
are unified, as above. <span class="note">(See there all this very carefully.)</span>
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Opening Passage]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">עַל-פִּי הַתּוֹרָה "וַיַּסֵּב אֱלֹקִים וְכוּ'" סִימָן סב, עַיֵּן שָׁם כָּל הַתּוֹרָה. וְהַכְּלָל בְּקִצּוּר לְעִנְיָנֵנוּ, שֶׁעַל-יְדֵי אֲכִילָתָם שֶׁל יִשְֹרָאֵל נַעֲשֶֹה יִחוּד קֻדְשָׁא בְּרִיךְ הוּא וּשְׁכִנְתֵּיהּ אַפִּין בְּאַפִּין וְכוּ'...</span>
</details>

<hr class="divider"/>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 1 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">1.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
All of this is of the aspect of <em>Pesach</em> and the <em>Exodus from Egypt</em>, for
<span class="ht">Mitzrayim</span> [Egypt] is of the aspect of <span class="ht">meitzar ha-garon</span>
[the constriction of the throat] — of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"my throat is parched"</span>, as above. For this was the essence of the Egyptian exile:
that speech was in exile, as is well known. And because of this the [festival] is called
<span class="ht">Pesach</span> — for it was then that the Exodus from Egypt occurred, when speech went
forth from the exile; and this is the aspect of <span class="em">Pesach</span> —
<span class="kab">peh sach</span> [<em>a mouth that speaks</em>], as is brought [in the holy writings].

For <em>Pharaoh</em> is the king of Egypt, whose name [in its Hebrew letters] spells
<span class="acro">הָעֹרֶף</span> [<em>ha-oref</em> — the nape of the neck]; and he had three ministers, as
mentioned above — the chief cupbearer, and so on — who correspond to the trachea, the esophagus, and
the veins, which are of the aspect of the lust for eating and drinking. For these three —
trachea, esophagus, and so on — are adjacent to the organs of speech, namely to the
<em>five articulatory places of the mouth</em> [<span class="ht">chameshes motzo'os ha-peh</span>].
Therefore when they overpower, they draw speech toward the nape of the neck, which is the aspect
of Pharaoh; and then it is the aspect of the exile of Egypt: that speech is in exile in Egypt in
the aspect of the constriction of the throat — of the aspect of "my throat is parched" — and one
cannot utter any word before Hashem, as above. And this is the aspect of the exile of Egypt that
[recurs] in every generation. And for this, fasting is needed, through which one cleaves to the back
of holiness, and so on, as above. And this is the aspect of the <em>prohibition of chametz</em>
[leavened bread] and the <em>mitzva of eating matzah</em>.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 1]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְכָל זֶה הוּא בְּחִינַת פֶּסַח וִיצִיאַת מִצְרַיִם, כִּי מִצְרַיִם הוּא בְּחִינַת מֵצַר הַגָּרוֹן, בְּחִינַת נִחַר גְּרוֹנִי כַּנַּ"ל. שֶׁזֶּה הָיָה עִקַּר גָּלוּת מִצְרַיִם שֶׁהָיָה הַדִּבּוּר בַּגָּלוּת כַּיָּדוּעַ...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 2 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">2.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
For the eating of Israel must be in great holiness, such that their eating be of the aspect of food
that is clarified — whereby through such eating they merit to unify the <span class="kab">Kudsha Brich Hu</span>
and His <span class="kab">Shechinta</span>, <span class="kab">apin b'apin</span> [face to face], as above.
But this is not merited except when faith is complete, as above. And in order to merit this, two
rectifications are needed, as explained in the abovementioned Torah-teaching: namely, to
<em>study the Poskim</em>, in order to open the mind [<span class="ht">da'as</span>] so that
the pathways of intellect not be blocked, so as to merit understanding — to resolve the difficulties
that can be resolved. And also one needs <em>fasting</em>, in order to bring forth speech from exile
and elevate the holy sparks. And afterward, speech turns its face and elevates the remaining sparks of
holiness, and so on. Then faith is completed in perfection and one is saved from the difficulties that
cannot be resolved — for which one needs only to strengthen oneself in faith. <span class="note">(As explained
there in the abovementioned Torah-teaching, well.)</span>

And then one merits the aforementioned eating of holiness, through which the <span class="kab">Kudsha
Brich Hu</span> and His <span class="kab">Shechinta</span> are unified, in the aspect of
<span class="kab">apin b'apin</span> as above. And these two aspects are of the aspect of the
rectification of <span class="em">da'as</span> [mind/knowledge] and
<span class="em">emunah</span> [faith]. For the rectification of the difficulties that can be resolved,
through the study of the Poskim, is of the aspect of the rectification of <span class="ht">da'as</span>
— whereby one merits complete <span class="ht">da'as</span> to understand what it is possible to understand,
to answer the difficulties that can be resolved. And the rectification of the difficulties that cannot
be resolved, through fasting, is of the aspect of the rectification of <span class="ht">emunah</span>,
for from those difficulties one can be saved only through faith, as above.

In truth, however, <em>everything is one</em>, and everything is included in
<span class="em">emunah</span> — <span class="note">(as explained in the aforementioned Torah-teaching)</span> —
for the essence of the rectification is <span class="ht">emunah</span>, as it is stated there in letter
<em>beis</em> [the second section], which begins: <em>"And the clarification of foods is through emunah…"</em>
For the truth is that Hashem has set a boundary for the intellect, and so on — see there.

It might seem somewhat difficult, for he [Rabbainu] opened with one thing and concluded with two
things: for he begins speaking only of <span class="ht">emunah</span> — that the clarification of foods
is through <span class="ht">emunah</span> — and then goes on to elucidate two aspects: the rectification
of <span class="ht">da'as</span> and <span class="ht">emunah</span>. For this explains that the difficulties
that can be resolved, which one merits through the study of Poskim, are of the aspect of the
rectification of <span class="ht">da'as</span>.

However, in truth, everything is one — for the rectification of <span class="ht">da'as</span> and
<span class="ht">emunah</span> are all one, and the two are interdependent. And the <em>essence</em>
is <span class="ht">emunah</span>, which is the foundation of everything, as it is written,
<span class="verse">"All Your commandments are emunah"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 119:86]</span>. Yet it is known that the vitality of <span class="ht">emunah</span>
comes from <span class="ht">da'as</span>, for <span class="ht">emunah</span> is of the aspect of
<span class="kab">malchus</span> [kingship/sovereignty], whose vitality comes from the
<span class="kab">ohr ha-panim</span> [the illumination of the face], which is
<span class="ht">da'as</span>, of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"in the light of the king's face there is life"</span>
<span class="src">[Proverbs 16:15]</span>.

For in truth, even though <span class="ht">emunah</span> is precisely to believe with faith alone,
without any intellect or reason — for that which the intellect grasps is not called
<span class="ht">emunah</span>, as explained in the abovementioned Torah-teaching —
nonetheless, the <em>root</em> and the <em>vitality</em> of <span class="ht">emunah</span>
is <span class="ht">da'as</span>. For certainly we cannot say of an animal, which has no
<span class="ht">da'as</span> at all, that it has faith; the essence of <span class="ht">emunah</span>
exists only with the human being, who has <span class="ht">da'as</span>.

Rather, Hashem graces us and draws upon our soul and our mind an illumination from a supernal
intellect of such [elevated] degree, that through this, the power of
<span class="ht">emunah</span> is drawn into us — for <span class="ht">emunah</span> flows in its
root from a supernal and very, very elevated intellect, and from there the power is drawn into us to
strengthen ourselves in complete faith.

And even though a person can merit complete <span class="ht">emunah</span> even without great
<span class="ht">da'as</span> — as we observe among common folk and women who have complete faith,
and this is the essence of <span class="ht">emunah</span>: to believe simply, as they do —
<span class="note">(as Rabbainu [Rebbe Nachman], of blessed memory, said elsewhere)</span> —
nonetheless, one who merits the completeness of sacred <span class="ht">da'as</span>, his faith
is stronger and stronger. And the more a person merits to increase his <span class="ht">da'as</span>
in holiness, in Torah and divine service, the more he merits to draw down a wondrous illumination
into <span class="ht">emunah</span>. And through this, his faith is strengthened increasingly
at every turn. For the more he knows, the more he understands that he does not yet know at all —
and that he must strengthen himself only in complete faith. And also, because he now sees that many
difficulties that once troubled his mind regarding Hashem and the tzadikim have now been resolved
for him, through the opening of his mind — through this he learns the hidden from the explicit:
that the difficulties which remain obscure to him certainly also have answers, only his mind has not
yet opened sufficiently to resolve those difficulties as well, or else they are of the category of
difficulties that cannot be resolved in this world in any manner whatsoever. And therefore he is
strengthened through this in greater faith at every turn.

It follows that through <span class="ht">da'as</span>, <span class="ht">emunah</span> is
strengthened — and, all the more so, the reverse: that the more a person strengthens himself in
greater faith, the more a great illumination is added to his mind. For the essence is
<span class="ht">emunah</span>. As is explained in the Torah-teaching <em>"And his hands were
[steady as] faith until the setting of the sun"</em>
<span class="src">[Likutay Moharan 1, Siman 91]</span> — that through
<span class="ht">emunah</span> one merits <span class="ht">da'as</span> — see there.

And therefore <span class="ht">da'as</span> and <span class="ht">emunah</span> are both one;
they are both included under the name <span class="em">"emunah"</span>, for the ultimate purpose of
<span class="ht">da'as</span> is <span class="ht">emunah</span> — of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"the ultimate of knowledge is that we do not know"</span>
<span class="src">[Bechinos Olam, 13:45]</span>.
And since the ultimate of <span class="ht">da'as</span> is "that we do not know," certainly
one need only stand firm in <span class="ht">emunah</span>. And this is the ultimate of
<span class="ht">da'as</span> that one attains: that one does not yet know anything at all —
yet through all the <span class="ht">da'as</span> one has attained, one understands with
wondrous contemplation how much one must strengthen oneself in <span class="ht">emunah</span>
and rely solely on faith, which is the foundation of everything.

And through this the difficulty we raised above is well resolved: Rabbainu, of blessed memory,
opened with one thing and concluded with two things — for everything is one, as above.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 2]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">כִּי אֲכִילַת יִשְֹרָאֵל צָרִיךְ לִהְיוֹת בִּקְדֻשָּׁה גְּדוֹלָה שֶׁיִּהְיֶה אֲכִילָתָם בְּחִינַת מַאֲכָל שֶׁנִּתְבָּרֵר... וְעַל-כֵּן דַּעַת וֶאֱמוּנָה שְׁנֵיהֶם אֶחָד...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 3 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">3.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And when one merits to complete faith through the abovementioned rectifications — namely, the study
of the Poskim and fasting — which are the rectification of <span class="ht">da'as</span> and
<span class="ht">emunah</span>, as above, which are both included in the rectification of
<span class="ht">emunah</span> as above — then <em>precisely</em> does one merit the eating of holiness,
through which one merits to unify the <span class="kab">Kudsha Brich Hu</span> and His
<span class="kab">Shechinta</span>, <span class="kab">apin b'apin</span>, as above. For the
<span class="kab">Kudsha Brich Hu</span> and His <span class="kab">Shechinta</span> are the
root of <span class="ht">da'as</span> and <span class="ht">emunah</span>, which are truly one, as
is known.

And therefore eating is called by the name of <span class="ht">emunah</span>, and also by the name
of <span class="ht">da'as</span>, as it is stated:
<span class="verse">"Graze on faith [<em>re'ay emunah</em>]"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 37:3]</span>, and it is written,
<span class="verse">"Eat, O beloved [<em>ichlu rei'im</em>]"</span>
[<span class="src">Song of Songs 5:1</span>] —
<em>two beloved friends</em> [<span class="ht">terein re'in</span>], and so on — who are
<span class="kab">chosam b'soch chosam</span> [a seal within a seal], of the aspect of
<span class="ht">da'as</span>. And as is explained elsewhere, eating is of the aspect of
<span class="ht">da'as</span> — for sacred <span class="ht">da'as</span> and <span class="ht">emunah</span>
are all one, as above.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 3]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְאָז כְּשֶׁזּוֹכִין לְהַשְׁלִים הָאֱמוּנָה עַל-יְדֵי תִּקּוּנִים הַנַּ"ל... וְנַחֲזֹר אֶל הָעִנְיָן.</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 4 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">4.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And in the Egyptian exile, both were in exile — <span class="ht">da'as</span> and
<span class="ht">emunah</span>. As is brought, in Egypt <span class="ht">da'as</span> was in exile.
And likewise speech was in exile, of the aspect of <em>heaviness of mouth</em>
[<span class="ht">keved peh</span>] — which is the aspect of the exile of <span class="ht">emunah</span>,
for the essence of <span class="ht">emunah</span> is through speech. <span class="note">(As is explained
elsewhere, and as is understood from the abovementioned Torah-teaching.)</span>

And therefore Israel could not exit the exile except through the two abovementioned rectifications —
namely the study of Poskim and fasting, as above. But in Egypt it was still before the giving of the
Torah, and they had not yet merited the abovementioned rectifications. And if they had waited until
they repaired those rectifications, they would have remained submerged in the exile, for the
<span class="ht">klippos</span> had seized hold of them so thoroughly that it would have been impossible
to draw those rectifications there.

Therefore Hashem did with us a great kindness and a wondrous miracle — He <em>leaped over</em>
[<span class="ht">pasach v'dilaig</span>] the appointed end, and shone upon us a wondrous illumination
that was <em>not in the natural order of things</em>. As is explained in the
<span class="ht">Kavanahnnos</span> [mystical intentions for Pesach] — from the immensity of the
illumination of the Pesach night, all the <span class="kab">mochin</span> [intellects/mindstates]
shine, and <span class="kab">mochin d'gadlus</span> [expanded intellect] comes before
<span class="kab">mochin d'katnus</span> [contracted intellect], and so on — see there — and through
this was the essence of the Redemption, and so on — see there.

That is to say: one must always first repair those abovementioned rectifications. And afterward speech
turns its face to gather the remaining sparks, through which the <span class="ht">sitra achra</span>
[the Other Side] is essentially subdued, and so on, as above. But now, at the Exodus from Egypt,
Hashem performed a wonder for us and illuminated the face of holiness with a wondrous illumination
<em>before</em> they had merited to repair those rectifications, for Hashem Himself was revealed in
Egypt — of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"I will pass through the land of Egypt — I am Hashem; I and not an angel; I and
not a seraph; I and…"</span>
<span class="src">[Haggadah, citing Exodus 12:12]</span>.
This was the aspect of the revelation of the face of holiness that shone then with a wondrous
illumination. Through this came the downfall of Egypt, until many of them abandoned their faith and
converted, as it is stated,
<span class="verse">"And also a great mixed multitude went up with them"</span>
<span class="src">[Exodus 12:38]</span>.

And through this came the Redemption. For the Redemption occurred on the first night of Pesach,
when on that very night Sarah was taken to the house of Pharaoh
<span class="src">[Genesis 12:15]</span> — this is an allusion to the splendor and beauty of
speech turning its face toward them, and so on, through which came their subjugation. <span class="note">(As
all this is explained in the abovementioned Torah-teaching — see there.)</span>

And because of this, chametz was forbidden on Pesach — <em>not to be seen, not to be found</em> —
and one must eat only matzah. For <span class="em">chametz</span> is of the aspect of
<em>difficulties</em> [<span class="ht">kushyos</span>] — of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"when my heart ferments [<em>yischametz</em>]"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 73:21]</span>, which is stated there in connection with the difficulties
that weighed on his heart regarding Hashem. And therefore throughout the year, eating is of
the aspect of chametz and matzah.
</p>
</div>
<p>
This is hinted at in the
<span class="em">Mah Nishtanah</span> — <em>"Why is this night different?"</em> —
with the statement: <em>"On all other nights we eat chametz and matzah."</em>
Now, seemingly it would have been sufficient to mention only that on all other nights we eat
chametz — as it says regarding other vegetables: <em>"On all other nights we eat any vegetable."</em>
Why did it also mention matzah? Rather, this alludes to the rectification of Israel's eating
throughout the year — for our eating is always, throughout the entire year, of the aspect of eating
chametz and matzah, for eating comprises the two abovementioned rectifications, which are the
rectification of <span class="ht">da'as</span> and <span class="ht">emunah</span>.

That is: to resolve the difficulties that can be resolved — this is the aspect of
<span class="em">chametz</span>, of the aspect of <em>leavening the brain</em>
[<span class="ht">chimmutz ha-moach</span>], of fermenting the brain and the
<span class="ht">da'as</span> to resolve and answer what it is possible to resolve.
And <span class="em">matzah</span> is of the aspect of the rectification of
<span class="ht">emunah</span>, which flows from a higher and higher supernal intellect as above —
so as not to gaze upon the difficulties that cannot be resolved, as above.

And therefore on Pesach — since we had not yet merited those rectifications, as above, but only a
wondrous illumination was drawn upon us from above, which is the aspect of the shining of the face
of holiness that shines then — the rectification of eating is drawn then only from there. And this
is the aspect of eating matzah, which is of the aspect of <span class="ht">man</span> [manna], as
above — as it is stated of it:
<span class="verse">"for they did not know what it was"</span>
<span class="src">[Exodus 16:15]</span> — for it is a most supernal and elevated holy eating,
beyond intellect, of the aspect of wondrous faith, as above.

But chametz is <em>forbidden</em> on Pesach even in the smallest amount — not to be seen, not to be
found — for it is forbidden to enter into any difficulty whatsoever, even those that can be resolved,
as above. But throughout the year chametz is permitted, for when one merits those rectifications,
then chametz is permitted — for then one merits to draw down the rectification of
<span class="ht">da'as</span>, to resolve the difficulties that can be resolved — which is the aspect
of the rectification of eating chametz throughout the year.

And therefore eating of holiness is always of the aspect of chametz and matzah combined, for it
comprises both abovementioned rectifications, which are the rectification of <span class="ht">da'as</span>
and <span class="ht">emunah</span>. But now on Pesach it is forbidden to leaven the brain with any
difficulty, even those that can be resolved — and therefore it is forbidden to eat even a trace of
chametz; rather one eats matzah, which on Pesach is drawn from the supernal illumination as above.

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 4]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וּבְגָלוּת מִצְרַיִם הָיוּ שְׁנֵיהֶם בַּגָּלוּת, הַדַּעַת וְהָאֱמוּנָה... אֲבָל עַתָּה בְּפֶסַח אָסוּר לְהַחֲמִיץ הַמֹּחַ בְּשׁוּם קֻשְׁיָא...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 5 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">5.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And therefore, after the seventh day of Pesach — which is the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds
[<span class="ht">Krias Yam Suf</span>] — chametz was permitted. For the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds
was through the staff of Moshe, and the staff of Moshe is
<span class="kab">Tat</span> [the Aramaic acronym
<span class="acro">מט"ט</span>] — <span class="kab">Metatron</span> — which comprises the
<span class="kab">shishah sidray Mishnah</span> [six orders of the Mishnah], which are of the aspect
of permitted and forbidden, and so on. This is the aspect of the study of the Poskim, through which
the sea of wisdom is split and one merits the rectification of <span class="ht">da'as</span> — to
resolve and answer the difficulties that can be resolved — and then <span class="ht">emunah</span>
is strengthened within us, as above.

And therefore afterward chametz was permitted — for the permitting of chametz throughout the year
is through the aspect of the rectification of <span class="ht">da'as</span>, through the study of
the Poskim, which is the aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds, as above. And therefore the
Splitting of the Sea of Reeds occurred on the <em>sixth day of the Counting of the Omer</em> —
corresponding to the six orders of the Mishnah, which are of the aspect of permitted and forbidden,
and so on; these are the essence of the rectification drawn through the study of the Poskim, which
is the aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds — through which chametz was permitted afterward,
as above.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 5]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְעַל-כֵּן אַחַר שְׁבִיעִי שֶׁל פֶּסַח שֶׁהוּא קְרִיעַת יַם סוּף הֻתַּר הֶחָמֵץ, כִּי קְרִיעַת יַם סוּף הוּא עַל-יְדֵי מַטֵּה מֹשֶׁה, וּמַטֵּה מֹשֶׁה דָּא מט"ט (מְטַטְרוֹן)...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 6 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">6.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
One must also on Pesach subdue the three ministers of Pharaoh mentioned above — the chief
cupbearer, the chief baker, and the chief butcher — who correspond to [those organs] as above.
And this is merited through the Pesach mitzva [slaughtering the Paschal offering], eating matzah,
and drinking the four cups [of wine] — which subdue the three ministers mentioned above. For the
mitzva of slaughtering the Pesach-offering subdues the <em>chief butcher</em>; the mitzva of
matzah subdues the <em>chief baker</em>; and the drinking of the cups subdues the
<em>chief cupbearer</em>.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 6]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">גַּם צְרִיכִין בְּפֶסַח לְהַכְנִיעַ שְׁלֹשֶׁת שָֹרֵי פַּרְעֹה הַנַּ"ל... כִּי מִצְוַת שְׁחִיטַת הַפֶּסַח מַכְנִיעַ שַֹר הַטַּבָּחִים...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 7 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">7.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And this is the aspect of the custom of the firstborn to fast on the eve of Pesach. For the essence
of the Exodus from Egypt — which is to bring speech out from the constriction of the throat and to
subdue Pharaoh and his three ministers, and so on — is merited through fasting, through which one
cleaves to the back of holiness, and so on, as above. And therefore <em>precisely the firstborn</em>
fast, for the essence of the virtue of fasting is because one returns each time to the beginning —
to when one first began to understand and to fast. And because of this, precisely through fasting,
one cleaves to the back of holiness — which is the aspect of returning each time backward, which
is the aspect of all the <em>beginnings</em> of holiness, for the <em>essence</em> is the beginning,
and so on. And each time one returns to receive strength from the beginning, and so on.
<span class="note">(As all this is explained there in the abovementioned Torah-teaching — see there well.)</span>

And therefore <em>precisely the firstborn</em> fast on the eve of Pesach, for the firstborn is the
<em>beginning</em> — it is the aspect of <em>first and beginning</em>. And therefore the firstborn
were sanctified, for the essence is the beginning — and when the beginning is sanctified, through
this the rest of the children and the subsequent offspring that follow him are easily sanctified as
well. As is stated in the writings of the Ari [<span class="ht">HaArizal</span>], of blessed memory:
that all the children who come after him receive from the soul-portion of the firstborn, and so on —
see there.

It follows that the firstborn is of the aspect of the beginning — which is the aspect of the
sanctity of fasting. And therefore the essence of the Exodus from Egypt was through the sanctity
of the firstborn. <span class="note">(As is explained there in the Parasha of Bo [Exodus 10–13].)</span>
For then Hashem slew the firstborn of Egypt and subdued the firstborn of the <span class="ht">sitra achra</span>,
and saved the firstborn of Israel, the holy firstborn. And then the firstborn were sanctified, as it
is stated there:
<span class="verse">"Sanctify to Me every firstborn…"</span>
<span class="src">[Exodus 13:2]</span>. For the essence of the Exodus from Egypt is of the aspect of
the sanctity of the <em>beginning</em>, which is the aspect of fasting — through which speech goes
out from the exile of Egypt and Pharaoh and his ministers, as above, which is the essence of the
Exodus from Egypt.

And therefore Pesach — which is the Exodus from Egypt — is truly the essence of the
<em>beginning</em>, for it is the start of Israel's drawing close to their Father in Heaven: they
began to recognize Him and began to draw near to Him at the time of the Exodus from Egypt on Pesach.
And then the firstborn were sanctified, for they too are of the aspect of beginning, as above.
And therefore it is customarily the firstborn who fast on the eve of Pesach — for the sanctity of
fasting is the aspect of the sanctity of the beginning, as above — through which the essence of
the aspect of the Exodus from Egypt [is achieved]: to bring speech out from the constriction of
the throat, and so on, as above.

And therefore <span class="em">Esav</span>, who despised the birthright, sold the birthright through
the lust for eating — precisely, as it is stated:
<span class="verse">"Let me gulp [<em>hal'iteini na</em>]…"</span>
<span class="src">[Genesis 25:30]</span>. For the sanctity of the birthright is of the aspect of
the sanctity of the beginning, which is merited through the breaking of the lust for eating —
precisely, which is the aspect of fasting, which is the aspect of the sanctity of the beginning.
And therefore the reverse: Esav, who despised the birthright, sold and despised it through the
lust for eating — precisely — as it is stated there:
<span class="verse">"And he ate and drank… and Esav despised the birthright"</span>
<span class="src">[Genesis 25:34]</span>. For it was precisely through the lust for eating and
drinking that he despised the birthright — which is the aspect of the beginning, whose sanctity
comes through fasting, through the breaking of the lust for eating and drinking, as above.

And <span class="em">Yaakov our forefather</span> merited the birthright then, for each one merited
his portion — for as soon as Yaakov saw the intensity of his [Esav's] lust for eating, and that he
said to him: <em>"Let me gulp…"</em>, Yaakov immediately said to him:
<span class="verse">"Sell me today your birthright"</span>
<span class="src">[Genesis 25:31]</span> — that is to say, as per the above: since you are so
submerged in the lust for eating, the birthright does not belong to you — for the sanctity of the
birthright is through the aspect of fasting, through breaking the lust for eating, as above.
And Esav agreed immediately, and sold and despised the birthright for the sake of the lust for
eating. And Yaakov gave him bread and a stew of lentils, and he ate, and so on, and he despised,
and so on. For Yaakov gave him all the lust for eating — for Yaakov himself had broken the lust for
eating, and gave the lust for eating to Esav, and for himself he took in exchange the birthright,
which is merited through the breaking of the lust for eating, which is the aspect of fasting, as above.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 7]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְזֶה בְּחִינַת מַה שֶּׁנּוֹהֲגִין הַבְּכוֹרוֹת לְהִתְעַנּוֹת בְּעֶרֶב פֶּסַח... וְיַעֲקֹב נָתַן לוֹ לֶחֶם וּנְזִיד עֲדָשִׁים...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 8 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">8.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And this is the aspect of the <em>Sefirah</em> [Counting of the Omer] — through which we count the
days and weeks and return each time to the beginning. For each time we return to the beginning of
the offering of the Omer, which was the essential <em>beginning</em> of Israel's drawing close
through <span class="kab">is'arusa d'lsata</span> [arousal from below]. For on the first night of
Pesach, the start of the drawing close was through the abovementioned supernal illumination — which
is the aspect of <span class="kab">is'arusa d'le'eila</span> [arousal from above]. But afterward —
after the first day — that illumination departs. And then we begin to count the Sefirat Ha'omer,
to begin to sanctify and purify ourselves in proper order, from day to day, of the aspect of
<span class="kab">is'arusa d'lsata</span> <span class="note">(as is brought in the holy books)</span>.
And throughout all the days of the Counting, we return each time to receive strength from the start
of the Counting — which is the offering of the Omer — as we say: <em>"Today is two days of the
Omer," "Today is three days of the Omer,"</em> and so on, for all of them [are counted in relation
to the Omer offering].

It follows that each time we return to the beginning — which is the offering of the Omer — and this
is the aspect of the sanctity of fasting: returning each time to the beginning. Through this we now
merit to go out from the impurity of Egypt and to rectify speech and to merit complete faith, through
the aspect of <span class="kab">is'arusa d'lsata</span>. For the essential rectification — to bring
speech out from the Egyptian exile, and so on — is merited through returning to the beginning.
And this is the aspect of fasting, and this is the aspect of the <em>Sefirah</em>, as above.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 8]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְזֶה בְּחִינַת סְפִירָה שֶׁסּוֹפְרִין הַיָּמִים וְהַשָּׁבוּעוֹת וְחוֹזְרִין בְּכָל פַּעַם לַהַתְחָלָה...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 9 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">9.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And this is the aspect of the <em>prohibition of chailev and dam</em> — in order to subdue and nullify
the two kinds of difficulties mentioned above: the difficulties that can be resolved, but for which
sometimes the pathways of intellect become blocked and it is impossible to resolve them — for which
the study of Poskim is needed; and the difficulties that cannot be resolved, which require one to
stand firm only in <span class="ht">emunah</span>, which is completed through fasting, through which
speech goes forth from the exile of Egypt, and so on — through which faith is completed, as above.

For the <em>prohibition of chailev</em> [suet/forbidden fat] is for the sake of the rectification
of <span class="ht">da'as</span> — so that the pathways of intellect not be blocked, to resolve the
difficulties that can be resolved. For <span class="em">chailev</span> is the <em>fatty substance of
the body</em>, through which the essence of the intellect is rectified. For the intellect burns like
a lit candle through the fatty substance of the body. <span class="note">(As is brought in the words of Rabbainu,
of blessed memory, elsewhere.)</span>

And therefore it is forbidden to eat the chailev of an animal [<span class="ht">beheimah</span>],
so that the chailev of the animal not overpower and block the <span class="ht">da'as</span> entirely.
For when the chalavin [suets] of the <span class="ht">sitra achra</span> overpower, the intellect is
completely blocked and one can — G-d forbid — forget Hashem, as it is written:
<span class="verse">"This is their way: folly is theirs"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 49:14]</span>.
And our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: <span class="verse">"They have chailev upon their sides [<em>kislayhem</em>]"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 73:7; see Talmud Berakhot 61b]</span>, and so on.
And this is the aspect of
<span class="verse">"Their fat [<em>chelbamo</em>] has closed [them in]"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 17:10]</span> — of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"for he covered his face in his fat"</span>
<span class="src">[Job 15:27]</span>.
<span class="em">Face</span> [<span class="ht">panim</span>] is the intellect — of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"a man's wisdom illuminates his face"</span>
<span class="src">[Ecclesiastes 8:1]</span>.
And through the overpowering of the chalavin, the intellect is enclosed and covered, until one
cannot resolve even the difficulties that can be resolved. And through this one forgets Hashem and
distances oneself from Him, G-d forbid, for the essential cause of distancing and forgetting Hashem
is through heresy [<span class="ht">kefirah</span>] and lack of faith, which flow from the difficulties
and the concealment of the knowledge of His divinity that is within every heart — <span class="note">(as
understood from the abovementioned Torah-teaching)</span>.

And therefore the Torah warned us against eating chailev — so that the intellect not be blocked and
sealed, G-d forbid — so that we merit to understand, to resolve the difficulties that can be resolved.

And the <em>prohibition of dam</em> [blood] is for the sake of the rectification of speech, which
is the rectification of <span class="ht">emunah</span>, as above. For the rectification of speech
is to bring speech forth from the exile of Egypt and Pharaoh and his ministers, and so on, as above.
And it is known that in Egypt during the exile there was an overpowering of the bloods [<span class="ht">damim</span>],
and this was the essence of the Egyptian exile — <span class="note">(as explained in the [kabbalistic]
writings)</span> — and this is the aspect of
<span class="verse">"I passed over you and saw you wallowing in your blood"</span>
<span class="src">[Ezekiel 16:6]</span>, and so on.

For speech comes from the blood [<span class="ht">dam</span>], for the blood is the life-force
[<span class="ht">nefesh</span>], and the essence of speech comes from the <span class="ht">nefesh</span>,
as it is written:
<span class="verse">"And the man became a living soul [<em>nefesh chayah</em>]"</span>
<span class="src">[Genesis 2:7]</span>, and the Targum renders:
<span class="verse">"a spirit that speaks [<em>ruach memalela</em>]"</span>
<span class="note">(as Rabbainu, of blessed memory, brings in many places)</span>.
And when the bloods of the <span class="ht">sitra achra</span> overpower — G-d forbid — then one cannot
speak at all, and one is then of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"I was dumbstruck in silence"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 39:3]</span> — the word <em>dumiyah</em> being of the language of
<em>damim</em> [bloods; cognate: <span class="root">ד-מ-מ / ד-מ</span>].

And therefore it is forbidden to eat blood — in order to subdue the evil bloods from which comes
the essential exile of Egypt, the essential exile of speech. And through being careful about chailev
and dam, one merits to rectify <span class="ht">da'as</span> and to bring speech forth from the
Egyptian exile — which is the rectification of <span class="ht">emunah</span>, as above.

And the essence is the rectification of <span class="ht">emunah</span>, as above. And the essential
rectification is through fasting. And this too is of the aspect of the prohibition of chailev and
dam, for through fasting the chailev and the dam are diminished — as we say after a fast:
<em>"May it be Your will that the diminishment of my fat and my blood be accepted before You as though
I had offered a sacrifice…"</em>
<span class="src">[Talmud Berakhot 17a]</span>.

And therefore, by not eating chailev and dam, this is of the aspect of the sanctity of fasting —
for <em>"one limb sustains another limb"</em>
<span class="note">(as Rabbainu, of blessed memory, brings elsewhere)</span>.
And if one were to eat chailev and dam, the chailev and dam within the person would increase.
But now, by not eating them, the chailev and dam are diminished — which is the aspect of fasting —
through which comes the essential rectification of <span class="ht">emunah</span>, as above.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 9]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְזֶהוּ בְּחִינַת אִסּוּר חֵלֶב וְדָם כְּדֵי לְהַכְנִיעַ וּלְבַטֵּל שְׁנֵי מִינֵי קֻשְׁיוֹת הַנַּ"ל... עַל-כֵּן עַל-יְדֵי שֶׁאֵין אוֹכְלִין חֵלֶב וָדָם זֶהוּ בְּחִינַת קְדֻשַּׁת תַּעֲנִית...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 10 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">10.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And therefore the <em>essence of the sacrifice</em> is chailev and dam. And the chailev ascends
upon the altar and is burned entirely as a pleasing aroma to Hashem, for chailev is the fatty
substance — the essence of the intellect. But we do not have the power to clarify the holiness and
the <span class="ht">kedushah</span> that is in the chailev of an animal — and therefore it is
forbidden for us to eat it with a severe prohibition. For anyone who eats chailev will have his
intellect completely blocked and the heresies and difficulties will intensify within him, G-d forbid,
as above.

But upon the altar, <em>precisely</em> the chailev ascends as a pleasing aroma, for the
<span class="kab">Bais HaMikdash</span> [Holy Temple] is of the aspect of <span class="ht">da'as</span>
and <span class="em">sechel</span> [intellect] — <span class="note">(as Rabbainu, of blessed memory,
said many times)</span>. And there, supernal intellect and very elevated <span class="ht">da'as</span>
shines. And therefore there, chailev ascends in the fire as a pleasing aroma — for precisely through
it, the intellect shines with great light to reveal His divinity, may He be blessed. For the burning
of chailev on the altar is of the aspect of the light of intellect — like a fire burning through
the fatty substance of the body, as above — except that we do not have the power to clarify it;
but upon the altar it is transformed entirely into holiness. And there, precisely through it, the
light of sacred intellect shines, to resolve and nullify all the difficulties that can be resolved —
and through this faith is strengthened, as above.

And therefore the chailev atones, for the essence of the blemish of all sins consists of heresies,
difficulties, and lack of faith, which sins cause — <span class="note">(as we have written many times)</span>.
And therefore through the sacrifice — through offering the chailev upon the fire on the altar,
through which <span class="ht">da'as</span> is opened and <span class="ht">emunah</span> strengthened,
as above — it atones.

And similarly <em>dam atones</em>. For in the <span class="kab">Bais HaMikdash</span>, the rectification
of <span class="ht">emunah</span> was drawn through the blood specifically. For blood is of the
aspect of the ultimate <span class="kab">tzimtzum</span> [contraction] and
<span class="kab">din</span> [judgment] — from which come the difficulties that cannot be resolved;
and therefore blood is forbidden for eating by a common person [<span class="ht">hedyot</span>].

But in the <span class="kab">Bais HaMikdash</span>, at the altar, through the <em>sprinkling</em>
[<span class="ht">zerikah</span>] of the blood of the sacrifice, the holiness concealed and hidden
within the blood is also clarified and rises. And through this, precisely, the <span class="kab">tzimtzum</span>
and <span class="kab">din</span> are sweetened, and <span class="ht">emunah</span> is revealed through
this — precisely — for this is the essence of the rectification and atonement for sins, which are
of the aspect of heresies, as above.

And therefore chailev is eaten and burned entirely upon the altar; but blood is neither eaten nor
burned on the altar — rather, it is sprinkled upon the altar, merely touching it. And similarly,
the remnants of the blood are poured at the base [<span class="ht">yesod</span>] of the altar —
they touch the altar and then go out, and are poured into the Kidron brook and sold for fertilizer,
and so on — as our Sages, of blessed memory, stated.

For chailev is of the aspect of <span class="ht">da'as</span>, from which come the difficulties that
can be resolved — only that, because of the hold of the <span class="ht">klippos</span> and the
<span class="ht">sitra achra</span> that cleave to the chailev of an animal, we do not have the
power to clarify it through our eating, and on the contrary: the intellect and <span class="ht">da'as</span>
would be completely blocked and sealed if it were eaten. But upon the altar it is burned in great
holiness, and then, precisely through it, <span class="ht">da'as</span> shines, as above — through
which comes the essential atonement, as above.

But blood is neither eaten nor burned on the altar — rather, it is sprinkled upon the altar, touching
it merely as a fleeting touch. For blood is of the aspect of the ultimate <span class="kab">tzimtzum</span>
and <span class="kab">din</span>, from which come the difficulties that cannot be resolved. And it is
explained in the words of Rabbainu, of blessed memory, in the Torah-teaching <em>"Come to Pharaoh"</em>
<span class="src">[Likutay Moharan 1, Siman 64]</span>: that these unanswerable difficulties flow from
the aspect of the <span class="kab">chalal ha-panui</span> [the vacated space], from which He contracted
and vacated His divinity, as it were, and so on — see there. And therefore those difficulties cannot
be resolved in any manner until the World to Come.

And therefore in the <span class="kab">Bais HaMikdash</span>, at the altar, a great rectification was
made through the blood — for the blood atones, because in the <span class="kab">Bais HaMikdash</span>
the intellect that will be revealed in the World to Come shines — which is called <em>"holy"</em>
[<span class="ht">kodesh</span>]. And therefore there, the holiness concealed even in the ultimate
<span class="kab">tzimtzum</span> — in the aspect of blood — is revealed and shines. And then
<span class="ht">emunah</span> shines with great light through the sweetening of the
<span class="kab">tzimtzum</span> and <span class="kab">din</span> of the <span class="kab">chalal ha-panui</span>
— through knowing that even there, His divinity is concealed — through offering the blood upon the
altar in the <span class="kab">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where the intellect that will be revealed in
the Future shines, as above.

And therefore blood is neither eaten nor burned upon the altar — for even though in truth the
holiness of His divinity is present even in the vacated space, in great concealment, it is in the
ultimate <span class="kab">tzimtzum</span>, in minimization, in great concealment and hiddenness.
And therefore blood is not entirely eaten upon the altar — rather, it is sprinkled on the altar
and touches it with merely a fleeting touch, then flows outside. For the holiness ascends from it
through the sprinkling, which is a mere touching of the holy altar — since the holiness in the aspect
of the vacated space, which is the ultimate <span class="kab">tzimtzum</span> of the aspect of blood,
is in the ultimate minimization and great concealment — of the aspect of a mere touching, as above.
But chailev is entirely transformed into holiness, as above.

And therefore it is entirely eaten and burned upon the altar. But <em>both</em> atone — blood and
chailev. And in fact, the atonement depends more on the blood than on the chailev — <span class="note">(as
explained in the words of our Sages, of blessed memory)</span> — for the essential rectification and
atonement comes through the rectification of <span class="ht">emunah</span>, which is more fully
rectified through the sprinkling of blood on the altar. For through this it is revealed that even
in the vacated space, in the ultimate <span class="kab">tzimtzum</span> of the aspect of blood, His
divinity is hidden and concealed — only in great contraction, of the aspect of a mere touching;
but nonetheless, even there, His divinity is concealed. And through this faith is strengthened —
and through this is the essence of atonement, as above. For the essence of the strengthening of
<span class="ht">emunah</span> must be to strengthen oneself against the heresies that come through
the difficulties that cannot be resolved — <span class="note">(as is understood from the words of Rabbainu,
of blessed memory)</span> — and this is rectified through the sprinkling of blood on the altar.
And therefore the essence of atonement is through the blood of the sacrifice, as above.
</p>
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Section 10]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְעַל-כֵּן עִקַּר הַקָּרְבָּן הוּא חֵלֶב וָדָם. וְהַחֵלֶב עוֹלֶה עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ... וְעַל-כֵּן עִקַּר הַכַּפָּרָה עַל-יְדֵי דַּם הַקָּרְבָּן...</span>
</details>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ SECTION 11 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">11.</span>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And this is the aspect of <span class="em">melichah</span> [salting the meat] — that one must salt
the meat in order to draw out all the blood. For salt is of the aspect of <em>fasting and sufferings</em>
[<span class="ht">tanis v'yisurin</span>], which purge a person's sins — as our Sages, of blessed
memory, stated: <span class="note">(Talmud Berakhot 5a)</span>
<em>"A covenant is mentioned regarding salt, and a covenant is mentioned regarding sufferings: just
as salt sweetens meat, so too, sufferings purge a person's sins."</em>
And sufferings are the aspect of fasting, which is self-affliction and suffering.

And as they brought there, in the adjacent passage, regarding the Land of Israel — which comes
through suffering — and they cited the verse:
<span class="verse">"For as a man chastises his son…"</span>
<span class="src">[Deuteronomy 8:5]</span>. And there that passage speaks of the matter of fasting,
as it is written in that context:
<span class="verse">"And He afflicted you and made you hungry and fed you the manna… and you shall know today,
for as a man chastises his son…"</span>
<span class="src">[Deuteronomy 8:3–5]</span>.
<em>"He afflicted you and made you hungry"</em> — this is the aspect of the affliction of fasting,
wherein one makes oneself hungry. And similarly our Sages, of blessed memory, stated in the
Midrash: <em>"He afflicted you and made you hungry"</em> — that they fasted before they ate the
manna, to purge the food that was in their intestines. For the manna is of the aspect of the
aforementioned eating of holiness, which one merits to unify the <span class="kab">Kudsha Brich Hu</span>
and His <span class="kab">Shechinta</span>, <span class="kab">apin b'apin</span> — which one merits
only through fasting. And therefore they fasted before eating the manna.

And therefore our Sages, of blessed memory, stated:
<em>"The Torah was given only to those who eat the manna"</em>
<span class="src">[Mechilta on Exodus 16]</span> — for the essence of the Torah is of the aspect of
<span class="ht">emunah</span>, as it is written:
<span class="verse">"All Your commandments are emunah"</span>
<span class="src">[Psalms 119:86]</span> — and this is merited only through breaking the lust for
eating through fasting, until one merits the holy eating that is the aspect of <em>manna</em>, as
above. For the one depends on the other: through the completeness of <span class="ht">emunah</span>
one merits eating that is from clarified food, and so on — <span class="note">(as explained there in the
abovementioned Torah-teaching)</span> — namely, eating of holiness. And similarly, the more one
breaks the lust for eating through fasting and sanctifies one's eating, the more one merits the
completeness of <span class="ht">emunah</span> — <span class="note">(as explained there — see there)</span>.

Let us return to the matter. It follows: fasting is of the aspect of sufferings, which are a
covenant of salt. And therefore one must salt the meat in order to expel all the blood — for this
is the aspect of fasting and sufferings that purge and expel from a person all the evil bloods
from which come all evil desires and all sins, G-d forbid, through which <span class="ht">emunah</span>
is blemished. For through fasting one subdues the <span class="ht">klippos</span> and the desires
that suckle from speech, and so on, which are the aspect of the exile of Egypt, and so on — which
are the aspect of the overpowering of evil bloods, which was the essence of the Egyptian exile.

It follows that fasting subdues and expels from a person the evil bloods — which are the
<span class="ht">klippos</span> and desires — which are the aspect of a blemish of faith. And this
is the aspect of salt, as above. And therefore one must salt the meat — for the subduing of the
bloods is through salt, which is of the aspect of the sufferings of fasting, as above.

And therefore one must salt in a <em>perforated vessel</em> [<span class="ht">kli menukav</span>] —
<em>precisely</em>. This alludes to the rectification of fasting: that the essential rectification
of fasting is to return to Hashem through it, and to examine one's deeds on the fast day, to
<em>open and perforate</em> [<span class="ht">l'nakev</span>] the heart of stone. As it is written
in Isaiah — and this is the Haftarah of Yom Kippur:
<span class="verse">"Is this the fast I choose?… Is this not the fast I choose: to loosen the bonds
of wickedness…"</span>
<span class="src">[Isaiah 58:5–6]</span>.
For the essence of fasting is to open and loosen the bonds of wickedness — to open and break one's
heart and extract from it all the knots of wickedness. And so wrote Rabbainu, of blessed memory,
in his rhymes [<span class="ht">charuzim</span>] printed in the Introduction:
<em>"Pierce the heart of stone and illuminate me from there…"</em>
<span class="note">(from the liturgical poem printed in the Introduction to Likutay Moharan)</span>
Thus he mentioned the rectification of the heart with the expression of <em>piercing the heart</em>.

And upon this alludes the perforated vessel: that one must salt the meat to expel the bloods in a
perforated vessel — <em>precisely</em>. But in an unperforated vessel, the salting is of no effect —
on the contrary, the meat becomes forbidden. This is an allusion: that fasting and sufferings —
which are the aspect of salt as above — avail only when one merits to open and pierce his heart of
stone, with intention toward Hashem, in order to return from his wickedness; for then through the
fasting one merits to drive out and expel the evil bloods — which are all the desires — and to merit
complete faith, which is atonement and rectification for all, as above.

<strong>Amen and Amen.</strong>
</p>
</div>

